# The Front End

The preceding material assumed the existence of an operation graph,
representing a calculation producing some desired result.  While such
graphs can be created at a low level (as is done in the tests for
instance), we would rather be able to specify arbitrary calculations
as easily as possible (not to mention without having to recompile the
program).  This is where front ends come in.


A front end is called upon to execute a program, which is typically
read from a file.  These programs are written in high level languages,
which are augmented with libraries of functions implementing the
various operations that can be added to the graph, along with types
that represent the data they operate on (polygons and polyhedra,
points and planes, transformations, etc.)

Invoking these functions during the course of the program instantiates
operations and links them into the graph appropriately.  Consider for
instance the following small Scheme program:

```Scheme
(import (gamma polyhedra) (gamma operations))
(define-output hemisphere (clip (sphere 10) (plane 0 0 1 0)))
```

The first line imports the libraries containing polyhedral primitives,
and operations.  From the former it uses the `sphere` function which
instantiates a `Sphere_operation` of radius 10, adds it to the graph
and returns it. This is then passed to the `clip` function, which
instantiates a `Polyhedron_clip_operation` and links it into the graph
as a successor to the `Sphere_operation` which is its first operand.
Its second operand is created using the `plane` function, which
returns a `Plane_3` representing the XY plane.  Finally, the result of
the clip operation is used to define the `hemisphere` output.

Once executed, the program will have resulted in the instantiation of
a simple graph with two operations (the plane is not an operation and
hence not part of the graph; it's simply passed to the clip
operation), which can then be evaluated to yield a hemisphere of
radius 10.
